# Probability refresher {#sec-probability}

Every model in this course is a statement about a random variable, and almost every
question we ask about one reduces to a single operation: taking an average. Before we
fit anything, it is worth pinning down what that average means and why it behaves so
well.

A distribution assigns weight to the values a random variable can take. @fig-distributions
shows three densities we return to throughout the course: a broad prior, a sharp
likelihood, and the posterior that balances them. Reading a density is a matter of
asking where its mass sits and how tightly it concentrates.

![Three densities that recur throughout the course: a broad prior, a sharp likelihood, and the posterior between them.](distributions.svg){#fig-distributions}

The single number that summarizes where a density sits is its expectation.

::: {.definition #def-expectation}
The **expectation** of a random variable $X$ with density $p$ is
$$ \mathbb{E}[X] = \int x\,p(x)\,\mathrm{d}x, $$
the weighted average of its values, each value weighted by how probable it is.
:::

By @def-expectation the expectation is linear, and that one property, applied to a
likelihood, is what the next chapter turns into an estimator.
